WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:02.669
You've now mastered an SDD workflow.

2
00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.638
Want something faster and lightweight?

3
00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.341
In this lesson, we will automate things

4
00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.344
but doing it our way with a custom process.

5
00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:14.347
We previously showed Agent Skills,

6
00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.950
an open standard to give agents

7
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.352
new capabilities and expertise.

8
00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:24.090
For example, we repeat the same prompt when starting a feature spec.

9
00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:27.594
Do this, do that, write these three files.

10
00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.797
Let's automate this with a skill

11
00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:32.799
and with help from the agent to write it.

12
00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:35.502
Ask the agent to use its skill creator

13
00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:37.704
to talk through this with us.

14
00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:41.207
As a note, there are many of these skill skills

15
00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:44.110
in the community that you can also install and use.

16
00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:47.847
As the agent runs, it might ask some follow-up questions.

17
00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:51.718
These are usually quite good. When the interview is done,

18
00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:54.421
we submit the responses and the agent proceeds.

19
00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:58.291
While the agent is working on the skill,

20
00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.826
keep an eye on the output.

21
00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.761
Is it making the choices you wanted?

22
00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.798
Success. As you can see,

23
00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:07.100
the agent wrote the skill to this directory.

24
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.004
As a note, skills can be per project or global.

25
00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.145
Skills can be invoked in several ways.

26
00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:23.149
In your prompt, refer to the skill before saying what to do.

27
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.152
Also, you can ask the agent

28
00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:28.455
to call a skill from another skill.

29
00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.325
According to the skills open standard,

30
00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.427
agents use the skill description

31
00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.629
to decide when to call it

32
00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:39.699
in a process called progressive disclosure.

33
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.602
But their judgment isn't always perfect,

34
00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.605
especially as the context window gets larger.

35
00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.675
Use the same heuristic as file tagging.

36
00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.878
If you know you want a skill used, name it.

37
00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.314
That saves you some thinking tokens.

38
00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:05.125
Agents have built-in / commands like /clear.

39
00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:08.428
Though initially popular, many agents are moving

40
00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:12.031
from custom/commands over to skills.

41
00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.236
Sometimes you need to give the agent more resources

42
00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:19.606
like access to some API, a private knowledge base,

43
00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.407
database, and so on.

44
00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:25.945
Until now the universal way to extend an agent has been MCP,

45
00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.548
Model Context Protocol.

46
00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:33.853
For example, agents need current quality context about packages.

47
00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:36.890
The most popular choice, Context7,

48
00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.193
an MCP that brings updated documentation of packages

49
00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:42.195
into your agent context.

50
00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.565
Now your agent can stay up to date with React 9.2

51
00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:49.169
and higher instead of React 9.0.

52
00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:52.138
MCP servers are still popular,

53
00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:57.138
but skills that use code tools like a CLI, command line interface,

54
00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:01.347
often accomplish the same purpose more elegantly.

55
00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.751
Context7 now suggests this, a skill

56
00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:08.188
that calls a CLI tool for Context7.

57
00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.991
Let's install the Context7 package for Claude Code.

58
00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:16.796
During the setup, we see this immediately.

59
00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:19.399
A choice between MCP server

60
00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:21.601
and CLI + Skills.

61
00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:23.870
We'll use the second choice.

62
00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.574
If this is your first time, you'll need to make an account.

63
00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:29.576
We already did so and logged in.

64
00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.779
Once done, we can go back into Claude Code

65
00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:38.017
and put it to use with an example prompt that uses Context7.

66
00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:40.687
As the agent runs, we see

67
00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:42.922
it detects the need to use Context7.

68
00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:45.925
When it completes, the agent shows

69
00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:49.329
it now knows how to find out information about our tech stack.

70
00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.933
This trend from MCP servers

71
00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:56.236
to skills plus CLI is accelerating.

72
00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.871
People are rethinking MCP

73
00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:01.574
because CLI tools can take action

74
00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:04.577
with less setup and less context usage.

75
00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.580
As you scale your workflow implementation,

76
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.515
skills, etc.

77
00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:13.319
You will want to share it with yourself, across machines,

78
00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:16.022
with teammates, perhaps with the outside world.

79
00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:20.326
Some agents such as Claude Code have plugins,

80
00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:24.330
a collection of agent extensions that can be installed and updated.

81
00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:27.333
There's a growing community of free plugins.

82
00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:31.838
Check them out to see if any will boost your SDD productivity.

83
00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:34.440
Remember, plugins are not yet

84
00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.542
a cross-agent standard.

85
00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:40.246
Like apps or dependencies, plugins can execute code,

86
00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:43.850
so make sure you trust them on install and update.

87
00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:48.354
GitHub's Spec Kit is one attempt at formalizing

88
00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.857
a spec-driven development workflow with agents.

89
00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:53.393
Installing Spec Kit for a project

90
00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:56.529
gives you access to / commands in your agent,

91
00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:59.165
similar to the workflow you used in this course.

92
00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:03.736
spec-kit constitution, plan, tasks, and implement.

93
00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:08.174
Another popular alternative is OpenSpec from Fission AI.

94
00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:13.574
OpenSpec follows a similar propose, explore, apply, archive workflow,

95
00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.984
where propose and explore match with the plan step,

96
00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:20.086
apply matches with implement,

97
00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:22.255
and archive matches with replanning.

98
00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:26.125
It also has canonical patterns for quick features.

99
00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:30.630
Both packages include helpful features like branch management,

100
00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:35.201
verification scripts, and opinionated spec document formats.

101
00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:37.737
I encourage you to experiment with these

102
00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:40.139
open source workflows to help refine your own.

103
00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.143
Sometimes in the middle of a feature, you have an idea.

104
00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:46.646
You want to research it with the agent,

105
00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:49.215
but you don't want to stop your branch work.

106
00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:52.985
For example, a choice of databases.

107
00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:56.089
But you're not yet committed to this idea,

108
00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:58.491
so you don't want it on the roadmap.

109
00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.564
The conversation produces some good ideas and some good questions.

110
00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:07.567
We accept most of the recommendations,

111
00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.769
but change our mind on one of them.

112
00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:12.038
You don't want to lose it.

113
00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:14.540
So, let's keep a backlog of research

114
00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.145
by telling the agent to write a report in a well-known location.

115
00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:27.186
This file is a record of your conversation and results.

116
00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:31.124
You can later ask the agent to schedule this research

117
00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:34.460
on the roadmap with a link to the backlog file.

118
00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:38.831
As this grows, you can write a skill to automate your research.

119
00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:43.136
Spec-Driven Development helps the agent write code your way.

120
00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:47.039
You can adopt an existing SDD framework or tool,

121
00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.544
then customize it using skills to operate your projects

122
00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:54.013
with your team, your way.
